{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"inheritance","title":"Inheritance: extends & super","group":"Prototypes & Classes","order":33,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>extends</code> sets up the prototype chain between two classes; <code>super</code> calls the parent's constructor and methods.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Extending a class</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Shape {\n  constructor(name) { this.name = name; }\n  area() { return 0; }\n  toString() { return `${this.name} with area ${this.area().toFixed(2)}`; }\n  static create(...args) { return new this(...args); }   // `this` = the called class\n}\nclass Circle extends Shape {\n  constructor(r) {\n    super(\"Circle\");           // must come before using `this`\n    this.r = r;\n  }\n  area() { return Math.PI * this.r ** 2; }                // override\n}\nclass Square extends Shape {\n  constructor(side) { super(\"Square\"); this.side = side; }\n  area() { return this.side ** 2; }\n  toString() { return \"■ \" + super.toString(); }          // extend parent method\n}\n\nconst shapes = [new Circle(1), Square.create(2)];\nfor (const s of shapes) console.log(String(s));\nconsole.log(shapes[1] instanceof Square, shapes[1] instanceof Shape);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Circle with area 3.14\n■ Square with area 4.00\ntrue true</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Rules for super</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Parent {}\nclass Child extends Parent {\n  constructor() {\n    this.x = 1;       // before super() → error\n    super();\n  }\n}\ntry {\n  new Child();\n} catch (e) {\n  console.log(e.name + \": \" + e.message);\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>ReferenceError: Must call super constructor in derived class before accessing 'this' or returning from derived constructor</code></pre></figure>\n<ul><li>A subclass without its own <code>constructor</code> gets <code>constructor(...args) { super(...args); }</code> automatically.</li><li><code>super(...)</code> only in constructors; <code>super.method()</code> in any method (also static methods).</li><li>Parent fields are initialised when <code>super()</code> returns; child fields right after.</li></ul>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-sitemap\"></i>Polymorphism and the chain</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Animal { speak() { return \"...\"; } }\nclass Dog extends Animal { speak() { return \"Woof\"; } }\nclass Puppy extends Dog { speak() { return super.speak() + \" (squeaky)\"; } }\n\nfor (const a of [new Animal(), new Dog(), new Puppy()]) {\n  console.log(a.constructor.name.padEnd(6), a.speak());\n}\nconsole.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(Puppy) === Dog);                    // static inheritance\nconsole.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(Puppy.prototype) === Dog.prototype); // instance inheritance</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Animal ...\nDog    Woof\nPuppy  Woof (squeaky)\ntrue\ntrue</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-brackets\"></i>Extending built-ins</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Stack extends Array {\n  peek() { return this.at(-1); }\n}\nconst s = Stack.from([1, 2, 3]);\ns.push(4);\nconsole.log(s.peek(), s.length, s instanceof Array);\nconsole.log(s.map((x) =&gt; x * 2) instanceof Stack);   // derived methods return Stack\n\nclass ValidationError extends Error {\n  constructor(field) {\n    super(`${field} is invalid`);\n    this.name = \"ValidationError\";\n    this.field = field;\n  }\n}\nconst err = new ValidationError(\"email\");\nconsole.log(err.name, err.message, err instanceof Error);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>4 4 true\ntrue\nValidationError email is invalid true</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-puzzle\"></i>Composition over inheritance</h3>\n<p>Deep hierarchies are brittle. Often it is simpler to <strong>compose</strong> behaviours: build objects from small functions or <em>mixins</em>.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const canFly = (Base) =&gt; class extends Base { fly() { return `${this.name} flies`; } };\nconst canSwim = (Base) =&gt; class extends Base { swim() { return `${this.name} swims`; } };\nclass Creature { constructor(name) { this.name = name; } }\nclass Duck extends canSwim(canFly(Creature)) {}\n\nconst d = new Duck(\"Donald\");\nconsole.log(d.fly(), \"/\", d.swim());\n\n// object composition with plain functions\nconst withLogger = (obj) =&gt; ({ ...obj, log: (msg) =&gt; `[${obj.name}] ${msg}` });\nconst service = withLogger({ name: \"api\" });\nconsole.log(service.log(\"started\"));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Donald flies / Donald swims\n[api] started</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why must super() be called before this in a derived constructor?</summary><div><p>In a derived class the parent constructor is responsible for creating the object. Until <code>super()</code> runs there is no <code>this</code> to use, so accessing it throws a <code>ReferenceError</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Does JavaScript support multiple inheritance?</summary><div><p>No — an object has exactly one prototype. You get similar results with mixins (functions that take a class and return an extended class), <code>Object.assign</code> of method objects, or composition.</p></div></details></section>"}